Sculpt the **Aegis**, a colossal six-legged walking fortress, as a static
120x110x150 smooth-surfaced model using only the `sn` meshing tool.

## How sculpting works

The only way to shape the surface is the `sn` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**.
Unlike a voxel tool that paints discrete cubes, `sn` maintains a **continuous
signed-distance field** — you **composite primitives** into it (adding material
with a sphere, box, ellipsoid, or cylinder; carving it away with another) and it
extracts a triangle surface from the field's zero level set. `sn` is the **Surface
Nets** extractor: it produces a **smooth, rounded, watertight, mid-fidelity**
surface with no sharp edges, at a fixed resolution; corners round by construction.

The field starts **empty**. Every primitive carries an **opaque** `#rrggbb` color —
there is no transparency. After each call the binary extracts the surface to
`mesh.glb` and re-renders a 3D preview to `model.png`, recording the operation. The
recorded operations — from which that surface is produced — are the authoritative
output that is judged; a surface produced any other way is discarded.
An empty field and a blank preview are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
sn add-box --x 60 --y 42 --z 74 --width 56 --height 22 --depth 84 --color "#c69a4b"
sn add-sphere --x 60 --y 84 --z 80 --r 13 --color "#c69a4b" --blend 3
```

Run `sn --help` to list every operation and `sn <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

